-- title:
--   ℤ[ζ₁₁] is a principal ideal ring
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a type carrying a field structure, assumed to be a number field (finite extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ in Mathlib's sense), and assume the typeclass hypothesis `IsCyclotomicExtension {11} ℚ K`, i.e. $K$ is generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by a primitive $11$-th root of unity (there exists a primitive $11$-th root of unity in $K$, and $K$ is generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the $11$-th roots of unity). The conclusion is `IsPrincipalIdealRing (𝓞 K)`: every ideal of the ring of integers $\mathcal{O}_K$ of $K$ is principal, i.e. generated by a single element. Since $\mathcal{O}_K$ is a Dedekind domain, this is the assertion that the class number of $K=\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_{11})$ is $1$; the Lean statement is, however, literally about principality of ideals and says nothing about class groups or class numbers. The statement is formulated for an arbitrary model $K$ of the $11$-th cyclotomic field rather than for a fixed construction such as `CyclotomicField 11 ℚ`, so it applies to any field satisfying the hypotheses.
--
--   This is the classical fact that $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_{11})$ has class number one, part of the standard table of class numbers of cyclotomic fields (see Washington, *Introduction to Cyclotomic Fields*). Formally it is phrased as `IsPrincipalIdealRing (𝓞 K)` for any field $K$ that is an $11$-th cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, rather than as a computation of $h(\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_{11}))$. It is used in the proof of [`fermatLastTheoremEleven`](thm.html#fermatLastTheoremEleven), the case $n=11$ of Fermat's Last Theorem, where unique factorisation in $\mathcal{O}_K$ (in the form of principality of ideals) replaces the general machinery.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IsCyclotomicExtension_Rat_eleven_pid.lean

